# Virtual environment
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Python caches
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.egg-info/
*.egg
build/
dist/

# Local database (regenerated daily by GitHub Actions)
*.db
*.db-journal

# Generated site (built by GitHub Actions, served from there)
dynasty_site/

# Local environment file (don't commit secrets)
.env

# Manual data drops (per-source CSV mirrors).
# We DO commit the computed-RAS CSV (generated by scripts/build_ras_csv.py
# from public nflverse data). Custom local exports stay ignored.
data/ras/*.xlsx
data/ras/*.tsv
data/ras/*_local.csv
data/cfbd/*.csv
data/cfbd/*.xlsx
data/cfbd/*.tsv

# v0.14.0: similarity comps cache is a build artifact — regenerated each
# launcher run when the similarity_career_arc source syncs. Don't commit
# (would just churn on every build).
data/similarity_comps_cache.json

# v0.15.0: per-format VORP / scarcity diagnostics — also regenerated by
# the similarity source each run.
data/similarity_vorp_debug.json

# v1.0+ engine output (regenerated by launcher / `run_engine(persist=True)`).
data/engine_v1/

# v2.0+ fantasy-arc corpus + sidecar (regenerated by launcher).
data/engine_v2/
# Exception: v2.4 ships a checked-in empirical era-pace snapshot so
# era-pace changes are reviewable in PRs (the rest of engine_v2/ is
# regenerated). See docs/V2.4-ERA-PACE-DELTA.md for the diff.
!data/engine_v2/era_pace_multipliers_v2.4.json

# v1.2.0 cohort diagnostics (regenerated on every engine run).
# Retained as a gitignore even though v2.0 removed the cohort diagnostic
# file — prevents accidental re-introduction if v1.2 caches are ever
# left behind on a dev machine.
data/diagnostics/

# v2.4 PFR scraper HTML cache (~60 MB). Source of truth is the
# normalised CSV at data/nflverse/player_stats_season_pre1999.csv.gz;
# the cache is regenerable by re-running scripts/build_pre1999_corpus.py.
data/pfr_cache/
data/sr_cache/
# v3.10 per-player parsed-career-stats cache (rebuilt from data/pfr_cache).
data/cache/pfr_career_stats/

# v3.0 sports-reference HTML cache. Cache only; the parsed/normalized
# data lives at data/sos/*.csv(.gz) and is regenerable by re-running
# scripts/build_cfb_sos_corpus.py. Shared with v3.0 PR 1 by convention.
data/sr_cache/

# OS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# Consensus snapshots: the full dated snapshot (~360 KB/day) is a build
# artifact, keep only the latest pointer + the crosswalk in git so the site
# builds offline.
#
# NOTE: this pattern is anchored to data/consensus/ and deliberately does NOT
# match data/consensus/history/ktc_values_*.json. Those are the compact
# (~6 KB/day) dated value records that make point-in-time transaction pricing
# possible on the Manager Score page, and they ARE committed -- the daily
# workflow appends one per run. Before "tidying" this rule into something
# broader, read dynasty/ktc_history.py: history cannot be backfilled, so
# ignoring it again would silently destroy the only copy.
data/consensus/ktc_[0-9]*-[0-9]*-[0-9]*.json

# v3.0 engine artifacts — regenerable by scripts/build_prospect_engine.py
# and scripts/build_prospects_v3.py during the launcher pipeline.
data/engine_v3/prospect_corpus.json.gz
data/engine_v3/prospects_*.json

data/tankathon_cache/
data/pfr_cache/draft_class/

# v3.11 Sleeper immutable-response cache for the cross-league corpus crawl.
#
# Completed NFL seasons never change, so their transactions, box scores,
# drafts and rosters are fetched once and kept forever -- that is what lets a
# daily run spend its budget on leagues it has never seen instead of re-buying
# history it already has (measured: 313 calls cold, 107 warm, for the same
# four leagues).
#
# NOT COMMITTED, for two reasons:
#
#  1. It is raw third-party data. Sleeper's API is free for non-commercial
#     use; caching responses to avoid hammering it is ordinary good
#     behaviour, but committing ~24 MB of verbatim transaction logs and box
#     scores into a public repository is republishing their data, which is a
#     different act and not one this project needs to make.
#  2. It is regenerable. Losing it costs calls, never correctness: the scores
#     themselves live in the `retained` block of corpus.json, which IS
#     committed, so an evicted cache means a slower crawl for a day, not a
#     smaller corpus.
#
# Restored in CI by actions/cache, exactly like data/pfr_cache above. The
# daily schedule keeps it inside the 7-day eviction window.
data/cross_league/cache/

# Per-league transaction audits behind the Best Managers drill-down, one
# gzipped file per league.
#
# Same reasoning as the cache above, and the same reason PR #72 removed the
# audit from corpus.json in the first place: this is bulk, derived, and
# rewritten whenever a league is re-scored. corpus.json is committed on
# every daily run, so an inline audit (2.3 MB for five leagues, ~56 MB at
# the corpus's current 122) would be paid again in git history every single
# day, forever.
#
# Losing this costs explanation, never correctness. The SCORES live in the
# committed corpus; an evicted store means the drill-down says "the
# pick-level record for this league is not in this build" until the crawl
# next scores that league. The page is built to say exactly that rather
# than render an empty panel that would read as no activity.
#
# Restored in CI by the same actions/cache entry as the Sleeper cache.
data/cross_league/detail/
